---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/site';
import { sitePath } from '../utils/paths';
---

<Base
  title="About | James Young"
  description="About James Young, a senior applied machine-learning and data-science leader building decision systems, interactive AI products, and scientific machine-learning systems."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <span class="page-path">ForetoData / about</span>
      <h1>The domain changes. The systems problem rhymes.</h1>
      <div class="page-intro">
        <span class="section-kicker">About James</span>
        <p>Across commercial operations, healthcare analytics, biotechnology, and computational biology, the recurring challenge is deciding what can be changed, what must be observed, and what evidence is strong enough to act on.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <article class="editorial">
        <p class="lead-paragraph">I am a senior applied machine-learning and data-science leader with a PhD in Biological Sciences, specializing in molecular biology. I build systems that make complex environments explorable, interventions testable, and analytical judgment reusable.</p>

        <h2>Represent the system</h2>
        <p>I start by separating the desired outcome from the variables that shape it: controllable levers, observed context, hard constraints, and uncertainty. That representation creates a common language for problems that otherwise look unrelated—from commercial resource allocation to fermentation conditions, protein sequence search, or private document intelligence.</p>

        <h2>Build the decision loop</h2>
        <p>Modeling is only one stage. The useful system compares scenarios, respects feasible ranges, recommends an experiment or intervention, and preserves a way to measure what happened. Industry work in causal inference, forecasting, targeting, and resource allocation taught me to treat adoption, decision rights, and feedback as part of the analytical design.</p>

        <h2>Expand the interface with AI</h2>
        <p>I build local LLM applications, governed natural-language analytics, adaptive analytical pipelines, and generative work-product tools. The goal is not to make the model sound authoritative. It is to give people a more fluid way to use complex systems while keeping computation, evidence, provenance, privacy, and approval boundaries explicit.</p>

        <h2>Keep domain depth</h2>
        <p>Scientific machine learning remains an important proving ground. My research connects comparative genomics, condition-specific multi-omics, protein language models, active learning, candidate prioritization, and validation planning. Those problems make uncertainty and expensive feedback impossible to ignore—and sharpen the same judgment I bring to operational systems.</p>

        <div class="text-links">
          <a class="text-link" href={profile.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('work')}>Selected work <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <aside>
        <div class="quiet-panel">
          <span class="meta-label">Compact profile</span>
          <ul class="question-list">
            <li>Scenario simulation and constrained optimization</li>
            <li>Causal inference, forecasting, and resource allocation</li>
            <li>Private local LLM applications</li>
            <li>Governed natural-language analytics and agentic workflows</li>
            <li>Adaptive analytical platforms and interactive products</li>
            <li>Protein language models, active learning, and multi-omics</li>
            <li>Peer-reviewed computational biology and scientific ML</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
